Title = {Integrated analysis of transcriptome and proteome provides novel
   insights into the response mechanisms of maca (Lepidium
   meyenii Walp.) to UV-B radiation},
Journal = {ECOTOXICOLOGY AND ENVIRONMENTAL SAFETY},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {300},
Month = {JUL 15},
Abstract = {UV-B radiation is a significant environmental factor that affects a wide
   range of biological processes in plants. Maca (Lepidium meyenii Walp.),
   a critical herbaceous plant native to high altitudes, shows strong
   adaptation to environmental stimuli including UV-B radiation. Despite
   the importance of maca for studying high-altitude adaptation,
   comprehensive transcriptomic and proteomic data on the response of maca
   to UV-B are lacking. This study used integrated comparative
   transcriptomics and proteomics to examine the gene and protein
   expression in maca leaves exposed to UV-B radiation for 0, 12, 24, and
   36 h. As a result, 7255 significant differentially expressed genes and
   257 differentially expressed proteins were identified following UV-B
   exposure. Bioinformatics revealed that UV-B radiation induced complex
   changes in the transcriptome and proteome of maca, affecting energy,
   carbohydrate, nucleotide, and glycan metabolism. Enrichment and
   protein-protein interaction network analyses highlighted the
   ``glutathione metabolism{''} pathway as significantly upregulated upon
   UV-B radiation. Quantitative PCR of eight genes associated with this
   pathway revealed that seven exhibited significant transcriptional
   upregulation, highlighting the critical role of this pathway in maca
   response to UV-B radiation. Glutathione content measurement and
   inhibition assays demonstrated that disruption of the ``glutathione
   metabolism{''} pathway led to a significant reduction in UV-B-induced
   total glutathione, reduced glutathione, and oxidized glutathione levels.
   This further supports the conclusion that the ``glutathione
   metabolism{''} pathway plays a crucial role in maca's response to UV-B
   radiation. The transcriptomic and proteomic data presented here provide
   valuable insights into the functional aspects and molecular response
   mechanisms of maca plants to UV-B radiation.},
